<picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/dynamic/json.svg?label=supported%20libraries&query=%24.badges.librariesSupported&url=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Foracle%2Fgraalvm-reachability-metadata%2Fstats%2Fcoverage%2Flatest%2Fbadges.json&style=flat&color=38BDF8&labelColor=1F2937\"><img alt=\"Supported libraries\" src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/dynamic/json.svg?label=supported%20libraries&query=%24.badges.librariesSupported&url=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Foracle%2Fgraalvm-reachability-metadata%2Fstats%2Fcoverage%2Flatest%2Fbadges.json&style=flat&color=0A7EC2&labelColor=E5E7EB\"></picture></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href=\"COVERAGE.md\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/dynamic/json.svg?label=tested%20library%20versions&query=%24.badges.testedLibraryVersions&url=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Foracle%2Fgraalvm-reachability-metadata%2Fstats%2Fcoverage%2Flatest%2Fbadges.json&style=flat&color=F59E0B&labelColor=1F2937\"><img alt=\"Tested library versions\" src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/dynamic/json.svg?label=tested%20library%20versions&query=%24.badges.testedLibraryVersions&url=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Foracle%2Fgraalvm-reachability-metadata%2Fstats%2Fcoverage%2Flatest%2Fbadges.json&style=flat&color=BF8700&labelColor=E5E7EB\"></picture></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href=\"COVERAGE.md\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/dynamic/json.svg?label=dynamic%20access%20coverage&query=%24.badges.dynamicAccessCoverage&url=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Foracle%2Fgraalvm-reachability-metadata%2Fstats%2Fcoverage%2Flatest%2Fbadges.json&style=flat&color=34D399&labelColor=1F2937\"><img alt=\"Dynamic access coverage\" src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/dynamic/json.svg?label=dynamic%20access%20coverage&query=%24.badges.dynamicAccessCoverage&url=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Foracle%2Fgraalvm-reachability-metadata%2Fstats%2Fcoverage%2Flatest%2Fbadges.json&style=flat&color=1F9D55&labelColor=E5E7EB\"></picture></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href=\"COVERAGE.md\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/dynamic/json.svg?label=tested%20LoC&query=%24.badges.testedLinesOfCode&url=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Foracle%2Fgraalvm-reachability-metadata%2Fstats%2Fcoverage%2Flatest%2Fbadges.json&style=flat&color=FB923C&labelColor=1F2937\"><img alt=\"Tested LoC\" src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/dynamic/json.svg?label=tested%20LoC&query=%24.badges.testedLinesOfCode&url=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Foracle%2Fgraalvm-reachability-metadata%2Fstats%2Fcoverage%2Flatest%2Fbadges.json&style=flat&color=C2410C&labelColor=E5E7EB\"></picture></a></p>\n85-  \n86-  This repository contains [reachability metadata](https://www.graalvm.org/latest/reference-manual/native-image/metadata/) for JVM libraries and frameworks, enabling them to work out of the box with [GraalVM Native Image](https://www.graalvm.org/latest/reference-manual/native-image/).\n87-  \n88-  The [GraalVM Gradle Plugin](https://graalvm.github.io/native-build-tools/latest/gradle-plugin.html) and [GraalVM Maven Plugin](https://graalvm.github.io/native-build-tools/latest/maven-plugin.html) automatically use the [reachability metadata](https://www.graalvm.org/latest/reference-manual/native-image/metadata/) from this repository, so most projects do not need to configure it directly.\n89-  \n90-  > [!TIP]\n91-  > **Missing metadata for a library you depend on?** Run the [Native Build Tools](https://graalvm.github.io/native-build-tools/latest/) task in your project to detect the gap and automatically open an issue here.\n92-  >\n--\n187-  observes a PR-eligible status; the dispatched workflows themselves are defined\n188-  separately, in §WF-forge-workflow-system and §WF-forge-workflow-architecture.\n189-  \n190-  The dispatcher routes issue work by issue labels, not by PR labels:\n191-  \n192-  | Issue label | Workflow driver | Successful PR label |\n193-  | --- | --- | --- |\n194-  | `library-new-request` | `ai_workflows/drivers/add_new_library_support.py` | `library-new-request` |\n195:  | `library-update-request` | `ai_workflows/drivers/improve_library_coverage.py`, or a missing-version compatibility repair driver | `library-update-request` |\n196-  | `fails-javac-compile` | `ai_workflows/drivers/fix_javac_fail.py` | `fixes-javac-fail` |\n197-  | `fails-java-run` | `ai_workflows/drivers/fix_java_run_fail.py` | `fixes-java-run-fail` |\n198-  | `fails-native-image-run` | `ai_workflows/drivers/fix_ni_run.py` | `fixes-native-image-run-fail` |\n199-  \n200-  The control plane treats a claimed issue as exclusive work. Claiming,\n201-  assignment checks, worktree creation, and final unassignment all belong in\n202-  `forge_metadata.py` and the GitHub helper layer rather than in individual\n203-  workflow engines. Workflow drivers should receive already-resolved\n--\n240-  which workflow engine, agent backend, model, prompt set, and workflow\n241-  parameters are used for the run. This keeps every workflow aligned with the\n242-  same repository, metrics, and local verification contracts\n243-  (§FS-local-ci-equivalent-verification) while letting operators select different\n244-  service profiles through configuration.\n245-  \n246-  The reachability repository must be present as a complete checkout or worktree\n247-  for every generated artifact. Forge does not run Gradle-backed testing,\n248:  dynamic-access reporting, metadata generation, or native tracing inside copied\n249-  per-library fragments. That architecture keeps generated tests, metadata,\n250-  Gradle build logic, stats, and Forge logs in one filesystem context.\n251-  \n252-  ## AR-forge-strategy-agent-boundary: Strategies configure workflows, agents edit code\n253-  \n254-  Registered workflow implementations own the generation loop. The codebase\n255-  currently names their base class `WorkflowStrategy`, but architecturally those\n256-  classes are workflow engines: they decide which prompt to send next, which\n--\n261-  \n262-  This boundary lets a predefined strategy bind a workflow engine, agent, model,\n263-  prompt-template set, workflow parameters, MCPs, and optional persistent\n264-  instructions without changing the workflow driver or the workflow implementation.\n265-  The workflow driver loads that bundle from `strategies/predefined_strategies.json`;\n266-  it does not hard-code model-specific behavior.\n267-  \n268-  Post-generation recovery is built into the workflow base class, not a pluggable\n269:  registry and not selected per strategy. When the post-iteration `./gradlew test`\n270-  still fails during finalization, the base class runs a fixed Codex-then-Pi lane:\n271-  a Codex metadata fix first, then Pi removing the offending failing tests as a\n272-  last resort (see the post-generation intervention glossary in\n273-  §FS-forge-functional-spec).\n274-  \n275-  Dynamic-access and native-test behavior stay in workflow specs, not in this\n276-  architecture file. The architecture only fixes the boundaries: workflow engines\n277:  call shared utilities for dynamic-access reports, and native test verification\n278-  is a reusable gate (§WF-native-test-verification-gate) that drives native\n279-  tracing and Codex recovery through the Gradle task contract in\n280-  §WF-native-trace-gradle-tasks. The concrete agent API and Pi adapter are\n281-  documented in §AR-agent-api, and the\n282-  strategy bundles that bind these pieces live in the strategy registry\n283-  (§STRAT-forge-predefined-strategy-contract, §STRAT-workflow-strategy-registry).\n284-  \n285-  ## AR-forge-verification-publication-boundary: Git scripts publish verified work\n--\n288-  metadata, index files, stats, metrics, and logs while it runs, but PR creation\n289-  is delegated to `git_scripts/make_pr_*.py` only after the dispatcher observes a\n290-  PR-eligible status, as required by §FS-local-ci-equivalent-verification and\n291-  §GIT-forge-publication. The publication script\n292-  stages the expected paths, writes a commit message with metrics context, opens\n293-  the pull request, applies the workflow-specific PR label, and links the result\n294-  back to the issue.\n295-  \n296:  This boundary is especially important for chunked dynamic-access work. A\n297-  non-final chunk publishes a reviewable PR that references the issue and carries\n298-  the exhaust-report state needed by the next run, as specified in\n299:  §WF-dynamic-access-exhaust-report. The final chunk is the only one allowed to\n300-  close the issue. The publication layer must preserve that issue linking\n301-  contract instead of treating every successful chunk as a completed issue\n302:  (§WF-chunked-dynamic-access-pr-linking).\n303-  \n304-  Shared repository edits are allowed only when local verification proves they\n305-  are necessary, and they must be surfaced in metrics and PR text for maintainer\n306-  review. Publication is therefore not a blind `git add .`; it is the point where\n307-  workflow-specific expected paths, labels, metrics, and human-intervention flags\n308-  become visible to reviewers.\n309-  \n310-  ## AR-forge-extension-points: New behavior plugs into one boundary at a time\n--\n330-  the behavior exists before adding architecture. Architecture declarations record\n331-  where a behavior lives in the implementation; workflow specs record the\n332-  operational contract; functional specs record the user-visible reason (see\n333-  §FS-forge-issue-resolution-goal).\n334-[tool-exec] end id=call_kle8ihvQkPytZow3izn7Lldh|fc_0ea73e23b74ebd23016a80526e63308191a1479d15a06b030c name=read status=success\n335-  # FS-forge-functional-spec: Forge functional specification\n336-  \n337-  This spec realizes the Forge direction set out in §GOAL-forge-direction, in\n338:  service of §GOAL-maximize-library-coverage and\n339-  §GOAL-shorten-issue-to-shipped-metadata.\n340-  \n341-  ## 1. Purpose\n342-  \n343-  ### FS-forge-issue-resolution-goal: Forge issue resolution goal\n344-  \n345-  The `forge/` directory automates the end-to-end resolution of supported GitHub\n346-  issue queues in the\n--\n362-  `fails-*` work comes from. Forge drains each pipeline by priority tier rather\n363-  than by scan batch: every `high-priority` issue first, then every `priority`\n364-  issue, then the remaining issues.\n365-  \n366-  ## 2. Scope\n367-  \n368-  The project covers these supported issue-resolution queues\n369-  (§FS-forge-issue-resolution-goal), in service of\n370:  §GOAL-maximize-library-coverage.\n371-  \n372-  1. **New library support** (`library-new-request`) — generate a JUnit (or\n373-     Kotlin/Scala) test suite, produce reachability metadata, and open a PR for\n374-     a previously unsupported library.\n375:  2. **Coverage improvement** — increase dynamic-access coverage of\n376-     already-supported libraries (via the `library-update-request` pipeline).\n377:  3. **Code coverage improvement** — planned workflow that creates new tests for\n378-     already-supported libraries and uses GraalVM PGO runtime profiles to broaden\n379:     ordinary API and execution coverage beyond dynamic-access call coverage, as\n380:     defined by §FS-forge-code-coverage-improvement.\n381-  4. **Java compilation fixes** (`fails-javac-compile`) — repair test sources that\n382-     no longer compile against a bumped library version.\n383-  5. **Java runtime fixes** (`fails-java-run`) — repair JVM-mode test\n384-     failures that surface when raising a tested library to a new version.\n385-  6. **Native-image runtime fixes** (`fails-native-image-run`) — update\n386-     reachability metadata so `nativeTest` passes against the new library\n387-     version, using the native metadata exploration phase.\n388-  \n--\n401-  automation bulk-tests newer upstream versions of already-supported libraries; it\n402-  records the versions that pass in a single `library-bulk-update` PR and files one\n403-  `fails-*` tracking issue per `(library, version)` whose update failed, labeled by\n404-  the stage that broke (`fails-javac-compile`, `fails-java-run`,\n405-  `fails-native-image-build`, or `fails-native-image-run`). Forge claims those\n406-  issues; it never opens them. The producer's contract is the reachability repo's\n407-  Library version update automation (§root/FS-library-version-update-automation).\n408-  \n409:  ### FS-forge-code-coverage-improvement: Code coverage improvement\n410-  \n411-  Forge should support a planned workflow that improves ordinary library runtime\n412:  code coverage, not only dynamic-access coverage (see\n413:  §WF-code-coverage-improvement), broadening the library coverage outcome\n414:  described in §GOAL-maximize-library-coverage. The workflow creates or improves\n415-  tests for libraries that are already present\n416-  in the reachability repo, uses GraalVM PGO runtime profiles as the execution\n417-  signal, and targets the whole practical public API surface over repeated runs.\n418:  Those tests must live in a separate code coverage test suite, not in the test\n419-  suite used to generate or validate reachability metadata.\n420-  \n421-  This functionality is distinct from the `library-update-request`\n422:  dynamic-access coverage workflow defined in §WF-improve-library-coverage.\n423-  Dynamic-access\n424:  coverage asks whether tests exercise calls that require reachability metadata;\n425:  code coverage improvement asks whether tests exercise the broader library API\n426-  and runtime surface. A strategy may chain both workflows, but each workflow\n427-  must keep separate metrics and review evidence so maintainers can distinguish\n428:  metadata-relevant coverage from broader behavior coverage.\n429-  \n430-  ## 3. Glossary\n431-  \n432-  | Term | Definition |\n433-  | --- | --- |\n434-  | **Reachability metadata** | JSON describing reflection, JNI, resource, serialization, and proxy access for a library, consumed by GraalVM `native-image`. |\n435-  | **Reachability repo** | Local checkout or worktree of `oracle/graalvm-reachability-metadata`. The build and metadata-generation Gradle tasks run inside it. The parent checkout of `forge/` is used by default. |\n436-  | **Forge metrics directory** | The `forge/` subdirectory of the reachability checkout, used as the transient staging area for a run's in-flight metrics (`.pending_metrics.json`) until the PR step reads them. Durable per-library run metrics persist to `stats/<group>/<artifact>/<version>/execution-metrics.json` (§FS-forge-run-metrics). |\n437-  | **Coordinate** | Maven coordinate of the target library, formatted `group:artifact:version`. |\n438-  | **Agent** | LLM-driven code editor (Codex or Pi) registered through [ai_workflows/agents/](../ai_workflows/agents/). Each implements `send_prompt`, `run_test_command`, and `clear_context`; the concrete API and Pi adapter are documented by §AR-agent-api. |\n439-  | **Workflow driver** | Deterministic script in the `drivers/` subdirectory of [ai_workflows/](../ai_workflows/) that prepares the working environment, directories, branch/context, strategy bundle, workflow engine, agent, and metrics for one claimed unit of work. The driver runs Forge plumbing; Codex or another LLM agent should not decide that setup during a generated run. Specified by §WF-forge-workflow-drivers. |\n440-  | **Workflow engine** | A registered state-machine-like workflow implementation among the core workflow objects in [ai_workflows/core/](../ai_workflows/core/), such as `basic_iterative` or `dynamic_access_iterative`. The engine owns prompts, command execution, retries, transitions, and terminal status selection for one run. |\n441-  | **Predefined strategy** | Named configuration bundle in [strategies/predefined_strategies.json](../strategies/predefined_strategies.json). It selects the workflow engine, agent backend, model, prompts, workflow parameters, optional MCPs, and optional persistent instructions. Selected via `--strategy-name`. |\n442:  | **Post-generation intervention** | The built-in recovery sequence the workflow base class runs when the post-iteration `./gradlew test` still fails during finalization. It is a fixed Codex-then-Pi lane, not a pluggable registry and not selected per strategy: a Codex metadata fix runs first (using the `fix-missing-reachability-metadata` skill, pinned to the run's GraalVM); only if that does not recover does Pi remove the offending failing tests as a last resort. When recovery makes the post-generation test pass, the run reports `SUCCESS_WITH_INTERVENTION_STATUS` and the intervention record (stage, intervention file, analysis) is saved for the run metrics and PR body. The base class runs this lane once per GraalVM test lane (current defaults and `future-defaults-all` on the latest GraalVM, plus current defaults on the GraalVM 25 toolchain). |\n443-  | **Human intervention** | A maintainer follow-up signal applied through the `human-intervention` issue or PR label when Forge has evidence that generated work, repository automation, or library execution semantics require human judgment. It is distinct from post-generation intervention, which is an automated recovery step. The policy is defined in §FS-human-intervention-policy. |\n444-  | **Dynamic access** | Reflection, JNI, resource access, serialization, or proxy use that GraalVM `native-image` cannot determine statically. |\n445:  | **Dynamic-access report** | JSON written by Gradle task `generateDynamicAccessCoverageReport` to `tests/src/<group>/<artifact>/<version>/build/reports/dynamic-access/dynamic-access-coverage.json`, listing classes and per-class call sites that require dynamic-access metadata, marked covered/uncovered. |\n446:  | **Dynamic-access exhaust report** | Durable coordinate-scoped JSON state for chunked dynamic-access work. It records the coordinate, issue number, class threshold, completed/skipped/exhausted/failed classes, and the latest published chunk PR/commit. It is stored in a stable location derived from the target test suite (for example under `tests/src/<group>/<artifact>/<version>/`) so orchestration scripts can find it from the coordinate alone. It does not predefine all chunks; each resume regenerates the current dynamic-access report and filters out processed classes. Specified by §WF-dynamic-access-exhaust-report. |\n447:  | **Chunked dynamic-access workflow** | Dynamic-access generation mode for oversized `library-new-request` issues and `library-update-request` issues routed to dynamic-access coverage improvement. `forge_metadata.py` owns the class threshold decision and passes the current chunk size to the workflow. The workflow processes at most that many uncovered classes, publishes that chunk, then resumes after the chunk PR merges. PR linking rules are in §WF-chunked-dynamic-access-pr-linking. |\n448-  | **Source context** | Read-only files supplied to the agent. Types: `main` (library source), `test` (upstream tests), `documentation` (Javadoc). Selected by the strategy parameter `source-context-types`. |\n449:  | **Library update target** | The metadata and test directories selected for a `library-update-request` coordinate (§WF-improve-library-coverage.3). Resolution records the requested coordinate, match type (`tested-version`, `metadata-version`, `default-for`, or `new-version`), matched index entry, resolved metadata version, resolved test version, and edit directories. |\n450-  \n451-  ## 4. Configuration Contracts\n452-  \n453-  ### 4.1 Top-level worker bootstrap\n454-  \n455-  - `do-work.sh` is a fixed bootstrap script and must not be changed for worker\n456-    behavior updates. It forwards `argv` unchanged to\n457-    `do_up_to_date_work.sh`, where every option and environment concern is\n--\n470-  ### 4.2 CLI inputs (common to all workflow drivers)\n471-  \n472-  - `--coordinates <group:artifact:version>` — required.\n473-  - `--reachability-metadata-path` — overrides default reachability-repo clone.\n474-    The Forge metrics directory is derived from it as the `forge/` subdirectory.\n475-  - `--strategy-name <name>` — selects an entry from `predefined_strategies.json`.\n476-  - `--docs-path` — additional read-only files for agent context.\n477-  - `-v` / `--verbose` — verbose agent output.\n478:  - `--keep-tests-without-dynamic-access` — only honored by dynamic-access\n479:    workflows (§WF-dynamic-access-workflow).\n480-  \n481-  The predefined strategy configuration contract — the required shape of every\n482-  `strategies/predefined_strategies.json` entry — is owned by the strategy\n483-  configuration architecture (§STRAT-forge-predefined-strategy-contract), not by\n484-  this functional spec.\n485-  \n486-  ### 4.3 Environment\n487-  \n--\n500-    may run concurrently. Valid values are `1` through `4`; the default is `1`.\n501-  - `FORGE_DO_WORK_STOP_FILE` overrides the shared stop marker path used by\n502-    `do-work` loops. The default is `~/.metadata-forge-stop`.\n503-  - `FORGE_USER_REQUESTED_ISSUES_ONLY=1` restricts issue queue scans to\n504-    user-requested issues by excluding configured automation and maintainer\n505-    authors locally before claim processing (§ORCH-forge-orchestration-spec).\n506-  - `FORGE_DYNAMIC_ACCESS_CHUNK_CLASS_THRESHOLD` configures the class-count threshold\n507-    used by `forge_metadata.py` for `library-new-request` issues and\n508:    `library-update-request` issues routed to dynamic-access coverage improvement,\n509-    and the post-repair exploration decision for `fails-javac-compile` and\n510-    `fails-java-run` issues.\n511-    The implementation-defined default is `15`.\n512:    If the current dynamic-access report has more uncovered classes than this\n513-    threshold, Forge uses chunked mode for new-library and library-update work.\n514-    Java-fix reports cannot be generated before their primary repair succeeds, so\n515-    `forge_metadata.py` passes the threshold to their shared driver; the composite\n516-    workflow evaluates it immediately after the repair and skips oversized\n517-    exploration. Publication then opens a new `library-update-request` for the\n518-    fixed version. That issue enters the ordinary library-update workflow after\n519-    the repair merges, where the existing dispatcher-owned chunking logic\n520-    regenerates the report and selects chunks. The skip is decided exactly once:\n--\n525-    creation, it recovers that issue from its repair reference. No other Java-fix\n526-    handoff state is persisted. For ordinary chunked runs, `forge_metadata.py`\n527-    still computes the concrete chunk size rather than passing a generic workflow\n528-    policy.\n529-  \n530-  ### 4.4 Repository availability for test and metadata artifacts\n531-  \n532-  - Every Forge workflow artifact that runs reachability-repo Gradle tasks for\n533:    testing, dynamic-access reporting, native metadata exploration, metadata\n534-    generation, or final verification must have the whole reachability repo\n535-    available. The runnable artifact must be rooted in a complete\n536-    `graalvm-reachability-metadata` checkout or worktree, not in a copied\n537-    per-library directory, extracted dependency artifact, or otherwise partial\n538-    tree.\n539-  - Complete repo availability includes access to the repo root, `gradlew`,\n540-    Gradle build logic, shared test infrastructure, `metadata/`, `tests/`, and\n541-    `forge/` when the workflow records metrics, logs, or resumable state.\n--\n553-  - **Durable session and generation logs** for every agent session,\n554-    generation attempt, deterministic Gradle command, metadata fixup, native\n555-    trace cycle, and local verification gate that contributes to the run, as\n556-    required by §FS-durable-generation-logs.\n557-  - **Generated tests + metadata + index.json** committed on a per-run feature\n558-    branch in the reachability repo, named `ai/<authenticated-login>/<suffix>`\n559-    where `<suffix>` is workflow-specific and encodes the coordinate (e.g.\n560-    `add-lib-support-<group>-<artifact>-<version>` for new-library support,\n561:    `improve-coverage-…`, `fix-javac-…`, `fix-java-run-…`,\n562-    `fix-native-image-run-…`, `not-for-native-image-…`).\n563-  - **Pull request** (only when invoked through `git_scripts/make_pr_*.py`).\n564-  \n565-  ### FS-forge-run-metrics: Per-run metrics record\n566-  §GOAL-minimize-generation-cost\n567-  \n568-  Every run must persist a per-library metrics record to\n569-  `stats/<group>/<artifact>/<version>/execution-metrics.json` so cost, token,\n570:  coverage, and status evidence stays attached to the library version it describes\n571:  (§GOAL-maximize-library-coverage). During a run, in-flight metrics are staged\n572-  transiently in the Forge metrics directory as `.pending_metrics.json` and\n573-  consumed by the PR step; they are not a durable output. Benchmark-mode runs\n574-  instead record durable, schema-validated metrics under\n575-  `benchmarks/benchmark_results/` (§BENCH-forge-generation-benchmarking.4).\n576-  Run metrics may identify the generated metadata artifact, but must not record a\n577-  single test-file artifact: the version-controlled test tree and pull-request\n578-  diff are the authoritative set of test files for the run.\n579-  When a failed run is preserved for continuation, `.pending_metrics.json` carries\n580:  the accumulated token, cached-token, cost, and iteration counters for the\n581-  preserved work. Any later resumed run for the same library must add its current\n582-  counters to the pending counters before writing durable metrics or publication\n583-  metrics, so repeated continuation attempts do not reset usage accounting.\n584-  \n585-  ### FS-durable-generation-logs: Durable generation and session logs\n586-  \n587-  Every Forge session and generation step must be logged and saved to a stable\n588-  path because durable logs are the primary debugging surface for generated work.\n--\n677-  paths. If any shared repository file changed, the PR must be labeled\n678-  `human-intervention` and the verification metrics and PR description must list\n679-  the repository-level paths that require maintainer review, following\n680-  §FS-human-intervention-policy.\n681-  \n682-  ### FS-library-update-tested-version-split: Library-update tested-version split\n683-  \n684-  A `library-update-request` entry often lists several tested versions of the same\n685:  library at once (for example `[\"1.1\", \"1.2\", \"1.3\"]`). When a coverage-improvement\n686-  run regenerates the JVM tests for such an entry, the new tests can pass on the\n687-  entry's own version yet stop compiling or running against a *later* tested\n688-  version. Forge must catch that break before the branch becomes PR-eligible and\n689-  split the entry, so the PR keeps the regenerated progress for the versions that\n690-  still pass while the repository keeps its existing support for the rest.\n691-  \n692-  **Version sweep.** Before publication (§FS-local-ci-equivalent-verification),\n693-  Forge runs a Java-only sweep. It runs `javaTest` for the changed coordinate once\n--\n738-  Forge's responsibility boundary, including:\n739-  \n740-  - Generated tests, metadata, or workflow edits fail local verification in a way\n741-    that points to the generated artifact or repository automation rather than a\n742-    transient external service.\n743-  - The workflow cannot converge after its configured generation, retry, and\n744-    recovery limits, and the saved logs point to a real library/test/metadata\n745-    problem that needs maintainer analysis.\n746:  - Dynamic-access coverage remains missing or suspiciously low after a\n747-    successful generation path, making the result misleading without manual\n748-    follow-up.\n749-  - Local CI-equivalent verification passes only after shared repository files\n750-    changed, so a maintainer must review repository-level effects before merge.\n751:  - Publication detects a severe metadata, test, or coverage anomaly that makes\n752-    the PR unsafe to auto-review as a normal generated result.\n753-  \n754-  Forge must not use `human-intervention` for failures that are only external or\n755-  transient infrastructure conditions. The issue-side classification is by failure\n756-  origin, not by log keywords: a workflow failure is **logical** — and gets the\n757-  label — when it comes from the driver script, the core workflow, or the local\n758-  CI-equivalent verification (compile, test, native-test, and finalization gates),\n759-  which includes the rare case of an agent timeout. A workflow failure is\n--\n808-  cause; stale, already-fixed, one-off, library-specific, or unsupported groups are\n809-  cancelled without opening an issue. Only groups backed by current code, prompt,\n810-  workflow, or policy evidence may open or reuse a tracking issue, so each live\n811-  system cause is addressed once instead of per source item. The automation only\n812-  investigates, groups, and opens tracking issues: it never edits, relabels,\n813-  closes, or merges the source items, so it preserves the labeling guarantees of\n814-  §FS-human-intervention-policy.\n815-  \n816:  Metadata-entry-count and coverage-depth objections for libraries that report\n817:  zero dynamic-access calls are never human-intervention on their own, because the\n818-  zero-call report does not provide enough information to decide whether metadata\n819-  is unnecessary or whether an entry-count drop is meaningful\n820:  (§FS-zero-dynamic-access-tolerance).\n821-  \n822:  ### FS-zero-dynamic-access-tolerance: Zero dynamic-access library tolerance\n823-  \n824:  A zero dynamic-access report means the current evidence did not observe\n825-  reflection, resource, proxy, serialization, or JNI call sites for the tested\n826-  version. It is not proof that the target coordinate and its transitive\n827:  dependencies need no reachability metadata: dynamic-access stats can miss\n828-  metadata required through downstream libraries. Review automation must therefore\n829-  treat metadata entry totals, metadata entry drops, and metadata-depth heuristics\n830-  as low-signal evidence for such libraries.\n831-  \n832:  When the PR evidence reports zero dynamic-access calls for the tested version\n833-  (for example `dynamicAccess.totalCalls == 0` in stats, or a `{}`\n834:  `reachability-metadata.json` with no claimed dynamic-access behavior), the\n835-  following must not be escalated to `human-intervention` or a requested-changes\n836:  review on coverage-depth grounds (§FS-human-intervention-policy):\n837-  \n838-  - A drop in total metadata entry count between the previous and new metadata\n839-    version, including a drop below the normal severe-drop guardrail. The zero-call\n840-    report does not give enough information to decide whether the entries were\n841-    unnecessary or whether the drop is a regression.\n842-  - A test that is shallow or exercises behavior that is not strictly the\n843-    library's responsibility, when that objection depends on metadata-depth\n844-    expectations inferred from the zero-call report.\n845-  \n846-  This tolerance applies to `library-new-request`, `library-update-request`,\n847-  `fixes-javac-fail`, `fixes-java-run-fail`, and\n848-  `fixes-native-image-run-fail` review labels (§FS-automated-pr-review). It does\n849-  not relax the label-specific gates that prove the submitted support or fix:\n850-  new-library tests must still be library-specific, compilation, JVM execution,\n851-  and native-image execution must still pass where the label requires them, and\n852-  reviewers must still reject tests that swallow the failing exception or disable\n853:  native-image behavior. It only removes metadata-entry-count and coverage-depth\n854:  objections when the zero dynamic-access signal leaves review automation without\n855-  enough information to judge metadata relevance.\n856-  \n857-  ### FS-automated-pr-review: Automated pull request review\n858-  \n859-  Forge review automation processes open pull requests by their PR labels after\n860-  CI has completed. It is a PR review workflow, not an issue-resolution workflow:\n861-  it must inspect an already published PR, use an isolated review worktree,\n862-  compare the PR diff and status checks against the label-specific review rules,\n--\n924-  ## 7. Run Status Semantics\n925-  \n926-  Every workflow records one of these statuses:\n927-  \n928-  | Status | Meaning |\n929-  | --- | --- |\n930-  | `RUN_STATUS_SUCCESS` | All generation gates and the local CI-equivalent verification passed; metadata and tests committed (see §FS-local-ci-equivalent-verification). |\n931-  | `SUCCESS_WITH_INTERVENTION_STATUS` | Tests succeeded after the built-in post-generation recovery modified the working tree (a Codex metadata fix, then Pi removing failing tests as a last resort), and the local CI-equivalent verification (§FS-local-ci-equivalent-verification) passed. The intervention's record is included in the run-metrics and PR description. PR-eligible; distinct from the `human-intervention` label unless §FS-human-intervention-policy separately requires that label. |\n932:  | `RUN_STATUS_CHUNK_READY` | A chunked dynamic-access run reached a reviewable class boundary and §FS-local-ci-equivalent-verification passed for the current part. The current part is PR-eligible, and the issue must not be resumed until the part PR has merged. |\n933:  | `RUN_STATUS_FAILURE` | The workflow could not converge or a quality gate failed; the feature branch is reset to its workflow recovery checkpoint and no PR is opened. Iterative dynamic-access exploration advances that checkpoint after each committed class (§WF-dynamic-access-fallback-and-failure); other workflows retain their specified checkpoint behavior. |\n934-  \n935-  The exit code is `0` for PR-eligible statuses and `1` for failure.\n936-  \n937-  ## 8. Chunked Dynamic-Access Semantics\n938-  \n939-  For `library-new-request` issues and `library-update-request` issues routed to\n940:  dynamic-access coverage improvement, `forge_metadata.py` must run or refresh the\n941:  dynamic-access report before choosing the execution mode. If the current report\n942:  has more uncovered dynamic-access classes than the configured class threshold,\n943-  `forge_metadata.py` must invoke the matching orchestration script with chunking\n944-  flags: issue number and current chunk size.\n945-  The current chunk size is normally equal to the threshold; when fewer\n946-  unexhausted classes remain than the threshold, it is equal to the remaining\n947-  class count. For example, with threshold `15` and `22` uncovered classes, the\n948-  first chunk size is `15` and the second chunk size is `7`.\n949-  \n950-  Chunked mode is automatic after the issue is marked with the\n951:  `chunked-dynamic-access` label. The normal project status remains the run-state\n952-  signal: `Todo` means Forge may claim the next chunk, `In Progress` means a chunk\n953-  is currently being generated or reviewed, and the final PR's `Fixes: #<issue>`\n954-  transition moves the issue to `Done`. If a non-final chunk PR has failed CI and\n955-  no failed-job rerun remains available, Forge must move the issue back to `Todo`\n956-  and mark that PR for human follow-up so a replacement chunk can be generated.\n957-  Forge must not require an explicit resume-state CLI flag; the exhaust report\n958-  location must be derived from the coordinate and loaded automatically by the\n959:  orchestration scripts, as specified by §WF-dynamic-access-exhaust-report. When\n960-  the issue is being resumed from a preserved failed-run continuation marker,\n961-  Forge may proceed without a coordinate-local exhaust report and use\n962-  `explore.exhaustedClasses` from the marker as the processed-class set for the\n963-  resumed run (§FS-forge-run-continuation.2).\n964-  \n965-  Chunk PRs use `Refs: #<issue>` until the final chunk. Only the final chunk PR\n966-  may use `Fixes: #<issue>` and move the issue to `Done`. Non-final chunk PRs\n967-  must commit enough exhaust-report state for the next run to skip classes already\n968-  completed, skipped, exhausted, or failed in earlier chunks\n969:  (§WF-chunked-dynamic-access-pr-linking).\n970-  \n971-  ## FS-forge-workflow-spec-catalog: Workflow specifications\n972-  \n973-  The whole workflow system contract is §WF-forge-workflow-system. Each supported\n974-  queue (Scope, section 2) is entered by a deterministic workflow driver\n975-  (§WF-forge-workflow-drivers) that prepares one run and delegates to the workflow\n976-  engine governed by a workflow spec. The driver scripts and the workflow specs\n977-  they run:\n978-  \n979-  | Queue | Driver script | Workflow spec |\n980-  | --- | --- | --- |\n981:  | `library-new-request` | `add_new_library_support.py` | new library support (§WF-add-new-library-support), which runs dynamic-access generation plus native metadata tracing and verification |\n982:  | `library-update-request` | `improve_library_coverage.py`, or the missing-version router | dynamic-access coverage improvement (§WF-improve-library-coverage), Java repair (§WF-java-fail-fix-workflow), or native-image run repair (§WF-native-image-run-fix-workflow) depending on the compatibility probe |\n983-  | `fails-javac-compile` | `fix_javac_fail.py` | Java failure repair (§WF-java-fail-fix-workflow) |\n984-  | `fails-java-run` | `fix_java_run_fail.py` | Java failure repair (§WF-java-fail-fix-workflow) |\n985-  | `fails-native-image-run` | `fix_ni_run.py` | native-image run repair (§WF-native-image-run-fix-workflow) |\n986:  | code coverage improvement (planned) | — | code coverage improvement (§WF-code-coverage-improvement) |\n987-  \n988-  Each engine is bound to a named configuration bundle defined by\n989-  §STRAT-forge-predefined-strategy-contract. The `basic_iterative` engine is not a\n990:  separate queue: it is the most basic workflow and the fallback the dynamic-access\n991:  workflow delegates to when a library turns out to have no dynamic access\n992-  (§WF-basic-iterative).\n993-  \n994-  Forge E2E testing is a top-level functional test contract because it validates\n995-  the whole issue-processing process through `forge_metadata.py`, not one workflow\n996-  engine in isolation, as described in §E2E-forge-workflow-testing.\n997-  \n998-  Forge benchmarking is a top-level benchmark contract because it compares\n999-  generation strategies across multiple `library-new-request` targets\n1000-  (§BENCH-forge-generation-benchmarking) and records cost (in service of\n1001:  §GOAL-minimize-generation-cost), token, iteration, LOC, coverage (in service\n1002:  of §GOAL-maximize-library-coverage), dynamic-access, and metadata metrics.\n1003-  \n1004-  The implementation roadmap orders the first known Forge spec gaps to close,\n1005:  starting with fixture-backed E2E coverage for the orchestration boundary; see\n1006-  §ROADMAP-forge-implementation.\n1007-[tool-exec] update id=call_2hHIJ2Dc6vwoCk8WJZbq81Ix|fc_0ea73e23b74ebd23016a80526e63448191b5409ef8f35f38ff name=bash\n1008-  ./metadata/org.springframework.shell/spring-shell-jline\n1009-  ./metadata/org.springframework.shell/spring-shell-jline/index.json\n1010-  ./metadata/org.springframework.shell/spring-shell-jline/4.0.2\n1011-  ./metadata/org.springframework.shell/spring-shell-jline/4.0.2/reachability-metadata.json\n1012-  ./tests/src/org.springframework.shell/spring-shell-jline\n1013-  ./tests/src/org.springframework.shell/spring-shell-jline/4.0.2\n--\n1147-  ./tests/src/org.springframework.shell/spring-shell-jline/4.0.2/build/test-results/test/TEST-org_springframework_shell.spring_shell_jline.Spring_shell_jlineTest.xml\n1148-  ./tests/src/org.springfra